In this lesson we'll look at how to pick the best measure of centre for a data set. Think of the mean, median, and mode as three different storytellers, each one highlighting something different. The mean, which is just the average, works best when your data is roughly symmetric and doesn't have any really extreme values, because it uses every number and gives you a fair picture of the typical amount. The median, that's the middle value, is your go-to when the data is skewed or when there's an outlier hanging out far from the rest. Since it just splits the data in half, a giant outlier barely nudges it. Then there's the mode, the most common value, which shines with categorical data, like picking the favourite ice-cream flavour in your class. The range is a quick spread check, handy for seeing how stretched out your data is. Here's the key idea: always justify your choice. If there's an outlier, say so, and pick the median. If it's nice and symmetric with no surprises, the mean is perfect. Now let's see it in action.